01:01:39.350 --> 01:01:42.310
And all he could get out was, I broke my nail.

1232
01:01:42.310 --> 01:01:45.570
And people were like, okay, did you break your fingernail?

1233
01:01:45.890 --> 01:01:48.530
He's like, no, guys, I broke my nail.

1234
01:01:49.170 --> 01:01:50.070
Oh, no.

1235
01:01:50.930 --> 01:01:51.750
Oh, no.

1236
01:01:52.990 --> 01:01:54.270
But he didn't leave set.

1237
01:01:54.490 --> 01:01:55.450
No, he didn't leave set.

1238
01:01:55.590 --> 01:01:56.670
They came over and they...

1239
01:01:56.670 --> 01:01:59.210
But there was that moment when someone was like, what happened?

1240
01:01:59.310 --> 01:02:00.330
Like Ed broke his nail.

1241
01:02:00.450 --> 01:02:03.350
And he's like, no, it's more than just a broken nail.

1242
01:02:03.510 --> 01:02:05.210
And then they wrapped it up.

1243
01:02:05.970 --> 01:02:10.550
Well, now, Angela, we're going to get into a big moment.

1244
01:02:10.910 --> 01:02:12.530
This was huge for our show.

1245
01:02:12.670 --> 01:02:13.810
This is a huge stunt.

1246
01:02:14.070 --> 01:02:18.750
You have to remember, our show never left a little tiny office.

1247
01:02:18.930 --> 01:02:20.990
So we've got to break down.

1248
01:02:21.130 --> 01:02:22.430
We've got two big things coming up.

1249
01:02:22.430 --> 01:02:27.030
We've got this Andy floating away and then the coals.

1250
01:02:27.330 --> 01:02:28.490
So Jenna, let's start with Andy.

1251
01:02:29.250 --> 01:02:29.490
Okay.

1252
01:02:29.910 --> 01:02:32.210
Andy walks over to the water.

1253
01:02:32.350 --> 01:02:37.110
And we had a fan question about this from Allie Jo Higginbotham, who said,

1254
01:02:37.730 --> 01:02:40.770
when Andy walks to the lake, he has something yellow in his hand.

1255
01:02:40.910 --> 01:02:41.650
What is he holding?

1256
01:02:41.890 --> 01:02:44.350
And what is he planning to do with it at the shore?

1257
01:02:44.830 --> 01:02:47.910
Well, he's holding a...

1258
01:02:47.910 --> 01:02:48.890
A bandana.

1259
01:02:49.130 --> 01:02:50.490
He's holding a yellow bandana.

1260
01:02:50.490 --> 01:02:53.190
Michael had handed these out in the egg race.

1261
01:02:53.290 --> 01:02:56.350
And that's what everyone had used to cover their eyes.

1262
01:02:56.510 --> 01:02:59.330
And Andy is holding his yellow bandana from the egg race.

1263
01:02:59.330 --> 01:03:00.370
And he's going to...

1264
01:03:00.370 --> 01:03:04.790
He hopes to dip it in the water to then maybe wrap it around his neck to cool himself off.

1265
01:03:04.910 --> 01:03:05.770
This was the idea.

1266
01:03:06.610 --> 01:03:06.970
Yes.

1267
01:03:07.130 --> 01:03:10.770
But instead, he falls in the water and starts floating away.

1268
01:03:11.410 --> 01:03:15.970
When he fell in the water, you know, I wasn't too far off because my character witnesses this.

1269
01:03:16.310 --> 01:03:16.790
Yes.

1270
01:03:16.790 --> 01:03:21.870
And there is a take that didn't make it that he literally went...

1271
01:03:21.870 --> 01:03:23.250
He like rolled, Jenna.

1272
01:03:23.570 --> 01:03:26.090
He like rolled into the water.

1273
01:03:26.430 --> 01:03:32.090
But they used this take because it was more clear why he was going down there.

1274
01:03:32.250 --> 01:03:36.090
He's trying to hang that bandana in the water, trying to balance.

1275
01:03:36.910 --> 01:03:44.310
Well, let me say, and we got another frequently asked question from Candice Kelso, Michael Loong,

1276
01:03:45.110 --> 01:03:47.610
Olive Taylor, and Phoebe Bono.

1277
01:03:47.950 --> 01:03:51.630
Was that really Ed in the water or was that a stunt double?

1278
01:03:51.870 --> 01:03:53.850
Guys, that was Ed in the water.

1279
01:03:54.110 --> 01:03:55.370
Ed did it all.

1280
01:03:55.570 --> 01:03:57.430
I don't know that they offered him a stunt double.

1281
01:03:57.750 --> 01:03:58.390
I don't know.

1282
01:03:58.990 --> 01:04:01.230
Kent told me they offered him a stunt double,

1283
01:04:01.230 --> 01:04:05.350
but Ed insisted on doing all of the floating in the water himself.

1284
01:04:05.750 --> 01:04:10.710
He was out there floating for over 45 minutes.

1285
01:04:11.250 --> 01:04:17.370
And I'm thinking maybe when he was like, oh, no, I'll fall in the water.

1286
01:04:17.730 --> 01:04:21.410
Like, maybe he didn't realize what that all entailed.

1287
01:04:21.870 --> 01:04:22.190
Yeah.

1288
01:04:22.190 --> 01:04:28.750
And perhaps having a stunt double on the side to complete some of the very far away floating.

1289
01:04:29.070 --> 01:04:34.030
But guys, that is Ed the whole time floating out there.

1290
01:04:34.210 --> 01:04:37.850
Not only in the day, but then he had to do it again at night.

1291
01:04:38.510 --> 01:04:42.210
This was two different shoots for him in the water.

1292
01:04:42.430 --> 01:04:44.210
A day scene and a night scene.

1293
01:04:44.770 --> 01:04:52.770
Now, when he was out there at night, he did have a thin wetsuit on underneath all that sumo gear to keep him warm.

1294
01:04:52.970 --> 01:04:54.150
So they did think of that.

1295
01:04:54.290 --> 01:05:00.710
But before we get too far into this stunt, Angela, we also had fan questions from Rebecca Gonzalez,

1296
01:05:01.530 --> 01:05:04.250
Connor N, and Maura Anderson, who said,

1297
01:05:04.490 --> 01:05:08.830
Angela, how did you make it through the scene of Andy floating away?

1298
01:05:09.770 --> 01:05:11.210
It was so hard.

1299
01:05:11.410 --> 01:05:12.730
It was, that's what she said.

1300
01:05:13.650 --> 01:05:14.370
Oh, boy.

1301
01:05:14.470 --> 01:05:14.930
I'm sorry.

1302
01:05:15.470 --> 01:05:17.090
It was really, I'm sorry.

1303
01:05:17.230 --> 01:05:18.250
It was really difficult.

1304
01:05:19.270 --> 01:05:20.770
Difficult not to laugh.

1305
01:05:21.210 --> 01:05:21.970
Really difficult.

1306
01:05:22.330 --> 01:05:25.710
Especially because Ed was improvising a little bit.

1307
01:05:25.830 --> 01:05:30.110
As he floated away, some of that dialogue was not scripted.

1308
01:05:30.270 --> 01:05:34.970
So, you know, when I'm like, I don't really understand what it is you're saying to me.

1309
01:05:35.150 --> 01:05:36.110
That was scripted.

1310
01:05:36.230 --> 01:05:42.610
But then Ed, Ed was physically floating away and he was dealing with the elements and he improvised this line.

1311
01:05:42.730 --> 01:05:47.570
Look at what I'm doing and go tell somebody it.

1312
01:05:48.050 --> 01:05:50.190
I love that line so much.

1313
01:05:50.390 --> 01:05:52.110
That was Ed improvising.

1314
01:05:52.790 --> 01:05:54.570
That is awesome.

1315
01:05:54.790 --> 01:05:55.190
I know.

1316
01:05:55.370 --> 01:05:56.330
So it was really hard.

1317
01:05:56.430 --> 01:06:00.190
And I didn't want to laugh because poor Ed is floating out in that water.

1318
01:06:00.330 --> 01:06:01.110
You know what I mean?

1319
01:06:01.670 --> 01:06:04.730
Yes, if you laugh, you ruin that moment.

1320
01:06:04.730 --> 01:06:05.110
Yes.

1321
01:06:05.210 --> 01:06:06.650
And he has to float longer.

1322
01:06:06.810 --> 01:06:07.650
I was working hard.

1323
01:06:07.650 --> 01:06:08.370
Oh, the pressure of that.

1324
01:06:08.490 --> 01:06:09.430
I was working hard not to laugh.

1325
01:06:10.310 --> 01:06:15.550
Well, we asked Jen about Ed in the water and here's what she had to say.

1326
01:06:15.790 --> 01:06:19.110
Oh, shooting the Andy floating away scenes was really fun.

1327
01:06:19.310 --> 01:06:21.030
I love that part of the script.

1328
01:06:21.030 --> 01:06:27.230
I love when Angela pretends that she doesn't understand what he's saying.

1329
01:06:27.230 --> 01:06:28.550
That was so much fun to write.

1330
01:06:30.090 --> 01:06:37.070
I remember that Ed was like in the sumo suit and then there was a rope attached to him and

1331
01:06:37.070 --> 01:06:42.330
he was being pulled by a boat and then at a certain point the boat would leave him and

1332
01:06:42.330 --> 01:06:43.530
then he would just drift.

1333
01:06:43.770 --> 01:06:45.090
But there was also a current.

1334
01:06:45.250 --> 01:06:49.910
I believe the water was also cold and he had to keep his head up.

1335
01:06:50.030 --> 01:06:54.490
And I remember that it was a challenging thing because his head would sort of get lost and

1336
01:06:54.490 --> 01:06:56.890
it would just like go under unless he helped.

1337
01:06:56.890 --> 01:07:00.470
He put his neck up and just try to hold it up.

1338
01:07:00.550 --> 01:07:01.950
So I think it was a difficult thing.

1339
01:07:01.950 --> 01:07:08.070
Also, the timing of the boat and the timing of us trying to get it on camera with Michael's

1340
01:07:08.070 --> 01:07:09.030
talking head was difficult.

1341
01:07:09.370 --> 01:07:14.530
So I remember that Ed was like a real trooper and there were a lot of discussions about

1342
01:07:14.530 --> 01:07:17.690
like how far out he should be at sea because we wanted to see him.

1343
01:07:17.930 --> 01:07:21.870
We didn't want to be too obvious that it was him and Michael might see him, but we didn't

1344
01:07:21.870 --> 01:07:24.270
want him to be so far away that he was this tiny little dot.

1345
01:07:24.490 --> 01:07:28.210
So Ed was in the water for quite a while while we tried to work this out.

1346
01:07:28.210 --> 01:07:34.150
Um, and the other thing I remember, which is crazy to say now, but there was a safety

1347
01:07:34.150 --> 01:07:35.450
meeting right before it.

1348
01:07:35.610 --> 01:07:42.650
And I, I feel like the very beginning of the safety meeting, like Ed was, Ed was there,

1349
01:07:42.850 --> 01:07:45.950
but like he was just getting there because everything was so rushed.

1350
01:07:46.130 --> 01:07:51.310
And I think the safety person said one thing, like as Ed was approaching that I wasn't sure

1351
01:07:51.310 --> 01:07:52.130
Ed clocked.

1352
01:07:52.190 --> 01:07:54.270
And I had this moment of, should I tell him or not?

1353
01:07:54.270 --> 01:07:59.850
And I remember the safety guy talking about water snakes and I was like, oh my God, oh

1354
01:07:59.850 --> 01:08:00.210
my God.

1355
01:08:00.350 --> 01:08:01.430
Do I repeat this to Ed?

1356
01:08:01.550 --> 01:08:02.450
Do I not repeat this to Ed?

1357
01:08:02.510 --> 01:08:07.010
I don't think I repeated it to Ed because like he was going to be in this water.

1358
01:08:07.190 --> 01:08:08.870
It was going to be very cold.

1359
01:08:09.430 --> 01:08:15.250
Um, I thought, would I want to know about water snakes before I got into the water in

1360
01:08:15.250 --> 01:08:16.290
a sumo suit or not?

1361
01:08:16.430 --> 01:08:19.230
So, um, yeah, I think I made that decision.

1362
01:08:19.650 --> 01:08:22.150
Tell me if you think that was the correct decision to make.

1363
01:08:22.150 --> 01:08:25.330
Anyway, so there were a lot of challenges of shooting that.

1364
01:08:25.530 --> 01:08:30.070
There were no, uh, snake events during the shooting of that sequence.

1365
01:08:30.070 --> 01:08:34.310
Uh, and I thought Ed did a phenomenal job and so did Angela.

1366
01:08:34.550 --> 01:08:41.510
And I'm just in love with that sequence of, um, events, but it was, uh, challenging to

1367
01:08:41.510 --> 01:08:41.750
shoot.

1368
01:08:42.770 --> 01:08:43.330
Wow.

1369
01:08:43.890 --> 01:08:48.250
Ed, Ed, possibility of water snakes.

1370
01:08:48.370 --> 01:08:49.250
Oh my God.

1371
01:08:49.690 --> 01:08:50.470
Oh boy.

1372
01:08:51.750 --> 01:08:52.790
Jen Salata.

1373
01:08:54.190 --> 01:08:59.770
Well, now Jen mentioned in her clip that they would have to pull Ed behind a boat to get

1374
01:08:59.770 --> 01:09:00.770
him in place.

1375
01:09:01.450 --> 01:09:05.410
So Kent told me that power boats were not allowed on the lake.

1376
01:09:05.710 --> 01:09:09.910
So they had to have a stunt coordinator in a row boat.

1377
01:09:10.070 --> 01:09:11.330
I remember, Jenna.

1378
01:09:11.690 --> 01:09:12.930
I remember rowing him.

1379
01:09:12.930 --> 01:09:19.770
So can you imagine if you're somewhere else at this park and you're just hiking, or maybe

1380
01:09:19.770 --> 01:09:24.870
you're riding a horse from the equestrian center and you look out onto the lake and you see

1381
01:09:24.870 --> 01:09:30.110
a man in a row boat rowing another man by a rope who's in a sumo suit.

1382
01:09:30.150 --> 01:09:31.850
What are you thinking when you see that?

1383
01:09:31.950 --> 01:09:32.430
I don't know.

1384
01:09:32.470 --> 01:09:33.430
What goes to your head?

1385
01:09:33.430 --> 01:09:33.830
I don't know.

1386
01:09:33.990 --> 01:09:34.630
I don't know.

1387
01:09:34.890 --> 01:09:35.790
Are you worried?

1388
01:09:35.790 --> 01:09:36.630
What are you thinking?

1389
01:09:36.630 --> 01:09:42.750
So I watched this play out because we would have to reset Ed to that spot where we would

1390
01:09:42.750 --> 01:09:43.450
have dialogue.

1391
01:09:44.090 --> 01:09:46.890
And inevitably, he'd float away, right?

1392
01:09:47.270 --> 01:09:49.910
And then they would, the guy, there he was.

1393
01:09:50.090 --> 01:09:53.190
And I had to make sure I didn't look at the rowboat guy, right?

1394
01:09:53.390 --> 01:09:57.750
You know, like I couldn't have my eyes dart away.

1395
01:09:57.750 --> 01:10:05.250
But off in the periphery of my vision was this guy in a boat and he would row and pull Ed.

1396
01:10:05.510 --> 01:10:15.550
Ed had a harness suit on under the sumo suit and they put through the arm of the sumo suit

1397
01:10:15.550 --> 01:10:16.930
a fishing line.

1398
01:10:17.430 --> 01:10:17.870
Yeah.

1399
01:10:18.330 --> 01:10:20.210
And this guy would pull Ed.

1400
01:10:20.210 --> 01:10:26.690
And as he did, Jenna, I would see water break around his body, you know, like if you pull

1401
01:10:26.690 --> 01:10:28.050
something in the water.

1402
01:10:28.710 --> 01:10:28.850
Yeah.

1403
01:10:28.970 --> 01:10:35.350
And Ed said that a majority of that water as he was being pulled would flush through the

1404
01:10:35.350 --> 01:10:35.790
suit.

1405
01:10:36.130 --> 01:10:37.030
Oh, my God.

1406
01:10:37.090 --> 01:10:38.250
And out his neck.

1407
01:10:38.570 --> 01:10:43.890
Well, Kent told me if you look carefully in the Michael talking head where Ed is floating

1408
01:10:43.890 --> 01:10:51.310
in the background, you will see that there's a pretty strong current in the water, but Ed

1409
01:10:51.310 --> 01:10:52.510
is not moving.

1410
01:10:52.590 --> 01:10:59.750
That is because at that point, the only way to keep him in the shot was to just keep him

1411
01:10:59.750 --> 01:11:01.130
attached to the boat.

1412
01:11:01.330 --> 01:11:08.550
And so he said, if you look for it, he's very oddly staying in one place while a pretty heavy

1413
01:11:08.550 --> 01:11:10.450
current is going underneath him.

1414
01:11:10.810 --> 01:11:12.390
So, so insane.

1415
01:11:13.770 --> 01:11:20.790
Angela, I also got the story about these sumo suits from Carrie Bennett, our wardrobe designer.

1416
01:11:21.210 --> 01:11:22.250
Oh, let's hear it.

1417
01:11:22.730 --> 01:11:30.270
So she said that this was one of the most difficult costume challenges she had for our show.

1418
01:11:30.450 --> 01:11:37.030
She had to create these inflatable sumo suits, but because she knew Andy would end up floating

1419
01:11:37.030 --> 01:11:43.450
away in it, whatever she put them in had to be safe for him to float on water.

1420
01:11:44.090 --> 01:11:48.410
So she said, as soon as she read the script, she immediately went to Greg and said, I cannot

1421
01:11:48.410 --> 01:11:51.190
design a costume that will allow someone to float on water.

1422
01:11:51.330 --> 01:11:53.210
I need a stunt person to help me.

1423
01:11:54.670 --> 01:12:00.710
So she got together with the stunt person and they realized that kind of like what Jen

1424
01:12:00.710 --> 01:12:06.710
said, the biggest challenge was keeping his head from going underwater because she was

1425
01:12:06.710 --> 01:12:10.210
like his body would float, but the head would go under.

1426
01:12:10.650 --> 01:12:16.710
So that is why the guys are wearing those crazy head pieces in the episode.

1427
01:12:16.970 --> 01:12:18.690
Those were custom made, right?

1428
01:12:19.230 --> 01:12:19.870
Yes.

1429
01:12:19.870 --> 01:12:29.450
She said, what if I made a floaty helmet in the shape of like a sumo wrestler's bun and

1430
01:12:29.450 --> 01:12:32.090
the bun is actually a floating device.

1431
01:12:32.570 --> 01:12:33.310
Brilliant.

1432
01:12:33.970 --> 01:12:40.170
So she designed this extra bit of safety for Ed with this floaty bun.

1433
01:12:40.330 --> 01:12:46.850
And before we shot, they went over to Kent Sabornak's pool and they put a stunt guy in

1434
01:12:46.850 --> 01:12:50.130
the suit and they tested it out and it worked.

1435
01:12:50.350 --> 01:12:51.350
Oh my gosh.

1436
01:12:51.530 --> 01:12:55.610
You're talking about what happens when like someone's riding a horse by and Ed's floating

1437
01:12:55.610 --> 01:12:56.870
and there's a guy with a rowboat.

1438
01:12:57.050 --> 01:12:58.430
What about Kent's family?

1439
01:12:59.210 --> 01:13:01.550
Hey dad, what's, who's in the pool?

1440
01:13:02.670 --> 01:13:03.790
Don't worry about it.

1441
01:13:03.970 --> 01:13:05.350
It's a little thing for work.

1442
01:13:07.350 --> 01:13:07.870
Totally.

1443
01:13:08.750 --> 01:13:09.270
Totally.

1444
01:13:10.430 --> 01:13:16.650
Also, when these guys in the writer's room wrote this sequence, they had

1445
01:13:16.650 --> 01:13:22.370
no idea the paces that they would put people through to make it realized on screen.

1446
01:13:23.170 --> 01:13:24.910
It was just a funny idea.

1447
01:13:25.190 --> 01:13:26.530
Oh, you know what would be funny?

1448
01:13:26.910 --> 01:13:31.250
They wrestle in these sumo suits and then Ed falls in the water and floats away.

1449
01:13:31.590 --> 01:13:34.830
I mean, the amount of effort that went into making that happen.

1450
01:13:35.230 --> 01:13:35.850
I know.

1451
01:13:35.950 --> 01:13:37.690
I think about this all the time with our scripts.

1452
01:13:37.830 --> 01:13:38.710
Like, oh, it'll be funny.

1453
01:13:38.850 --> 01:13:40.170
We'll have a funeral for a bird.

1454
01:13:40.230 --> 01:13:43.070
But we need a specialized tiny coffin made.

1455
01:13:43.530 --> 01:13:43.970
Yeah.

1456
01:13:43.970 --> 01:13:46.390
And several dead birds to choose from.

1457
01:13:46.570 --> 01:13:46.810
Mm-hmm.

1458
01:13:47.450 --> 01:13:51.170
And then Jim and Karen sneak off and they're sneaking off to call David.

1459
01:13:51.310 --> 01:13:52.370
But Pam doesn't know that.

1460
01:13:52.370 --> 01:13:54.850
But you know she saw them walk away.

1461
01:13:55.030 --> 01:13:55.750
You know she did.

1462
01:13:55.950 --> 01:13:56.710
She's aware.

1463
01:13:56.890 --> 01:13:56.990
Yeah.

1464
01:13:57.390 --> 01:14:00.950
And they have this moment where they, you know, call David.

1465
01:14:01.130 --> 01:14:02.450
Jim says he wants to be considered.

1466
01:14:02.450 --> 01:14:03.810
And then Karen does.

1467
01:14:04.030 --> 01:14:09.730
And then Jim is, like, teasing her because she sort of fumbles her words, right, when she calls David.

1468
01:14:10.710 --> 01:14:11.110
Yeah.

1469
01:14:11.210 --> 01:14:13.990
I didn't think that was cool boyfriend behavior, I have to say.

1470
01:14:14.190 --> 01:14:14.610
Oh, right.

1471
01:14:14.650 --> 01:14:17.450
Because she's trying to leave a professional message, right?

1472
01:14:18.690 --> 01:14:20.950
She's speaking to David Wallace.

1473
01:14:21.150 --> 01:14:24.650
And she's saying she'd like to be considered for this job.

1474
01:14:24.750 --> 01:14:28.890
And he's kind of heckling her during this phone call.

1475
01:14:29.130 --> 01:14:32.150
And I, and he's distracting her.

1476
01:14:32.390 --> 01:14:36.270
And I know I think it was meant to be kind of, like, cute and playful.

1477
01:14:36.630 --> 01:14:38.810
But I don't know.

1478
01:14:39.710 --> 01:14:45.350
If I'm, if I'm making a professional phone call, I don't appreciate being heckled.

1479
01:14:45.550 --> 01:14:46.430
Don't mess with me.

1480
01:14:47.130 --> 01:14:50.770
Don't mess with me when I'm making an important boss lady call.

1481
01:14:51.030 --> 01:14:53.230
Or is he trying to sabotage?

1482
01:14:53.870 --> 01:14:56.410
He might be trying to sandwich this moment.

1483
01:14:56.630 --> 01:14:57.770
He tried to sandwich it.

1484
01:14:58.290 --> 01:15:02.010
Well, we also mentioned earlier that this was an added scene.

1485
01:15:03.170 --> 01:15:06.590
Originally, these phone calls happened in the office before we went to Beach Day.

1486
01:15:06.590 --> 01:15:08.470
But they made a change.

1487
01:15:08.670 --> 01:15:11.850
And they decided it was better to reveal this information later.

1488
01:15:12.290 --> 01:15:15.650
But this led to probleMs.

1489
01:15:15.650 --> 01:15:18.050
Like, we already had such a packed day.

1490
01:15:18.070 --> 01:15:21.330
And here we were trying to add little moments and add little scenes.

1491
01:15:21.530 --> 01:15:25.030
And Jen actually talked about this as one of the challenges.

1492
01:15:25.030 --> 01:15:28.650
Just trying to get everything shot on these days.

1493
01:15:28.710 --> 01:15:31.810
Because, you know, you have so many things you have to shoot in daylight.

1494
01:15:31.810 --> 01:15:33.630
And then the sun goes down.

1495
01:15:33.630 --> 01:15:35.110
You can't make it stay out.

1496
01:15:35.110 --> 01:15:37.350
Also, these were such big, ambitious days.

1497
01:15:37.350 --> 01:15:45.150
And if we would go past the allotted time, we would get into overtime, which was incredibly expensive for the show.

1498
01:15:45.150 --> 01:15:48.470
Because they would have to pay the crew overtime, the actors overtime.

1499
01:15:48.790 --> 01:15:53.570
And so Kent Zabornak, our line producer, was trying to keep us on our day.

1500
01:15:53.750 --> 01:15:56.710
And he was having to check in with Jen and Harold quite a bit about it.

1501
01:15:57.450 --> 01:15:57.890
Yeah.

1502
01:15:58.110 --> 01:15:59.590
Here's what she had to say about that.

1503
01:15:59.590 --> 01:16:05.770
And I remember Kent being very patient with us and giving us extra time and adding in cushions.

1504
01:16:05.970 --> 01:16:10.290
But it was still really difficult to get everything that we needed to get done.

1505
01:16:10.530 --> 01:16:12.970
I mean, we were going very fast and working incredibly efficiently.

1506
01:16:12.970 --> 01:16:14.230
It was just hard.

1507
01:16:14.230 --> 01:16:22.710
And I remember there was one moment where Kent had already given us a cushion and maybe even a second cushion.

1508
01:16:22.710 --> 01:16:27.070
And then we were about to go over it.

1509
01:16:27.150 --> 01:16:28.490
But we were almost done.

1510
01:16:28.610 --> 01:16:29.730
We were so close to being done.

1511
01:16:29.730 --> 01:16:37.490
But Kent called and I got the call and we were about ready to say, you know, Harold was about ready to call action.

1512
01:16:37.930 --> 01:16:46.130
And Kent is, like, frustrated because he wants us to be done because he's given us extra time and he wants us to be done and completely understandable.

1513
01:16:46.410 --> 01:16:54.350
But I knew that if I kept talking to Kent, we were going to be slowed down by 10 seconds at that point.

1514
01:16:54.470 --> 01:16:57.250
And that 10 seconds would allow us to finish the shot.

1515
01:16:57.250 --> 01:17:07.490
So Kent is talking to me and I very gently and gingerly just put the phone down on a chair and we got the last shot and we were done.

1516
01:17:07.870 --> 01:17:14.710
It was definitely this weird moment of, like, this is the producer of your show telling you that you need to stop shooting.

1517
01:17:14.710 --> 01:17:19.470
And I realized I can either talk to him about the fact that we're going to stop shooting or get the shot.

1518
01:17:19.650 --> 01:17:22.430
So we got the shot.

1519
01:17:22.610 --> 01:17:26.150
It was definitely one of those moments of, like, hope that was cool.

1520
01:17:26.150 --> 01:17:33.650
Well, Angela, I think that what Jen is talking about is a little bit of sabotage.

1521
01:17:33.890 --> 01:17:34.990
Is she sabotaging?

1522
01:17:36.210 --> 01:17:37.750
She's not sabotaging.

1523
01:17:37.990 --> 01:17:39.710
She's sabotaging her phone call.

1524
01:17:39.970 --> 01:17:40.410
Right.

1525
01:17:40.590 --> 01:17:42.270
So that they could get these shots.

1526
01:17:42.830 --> 01:17:44.270
I think it's pretty amazing.

1527
01:17:44.510 --> 01:17:48.530
Like, I can sort of just visualize Kent going on and on like, Jen, you can't keep adding scenes.

1528
01:17:48.650 --> 01:17:52.690
And she just set the phone down and was like, yes, do it one more time.

1529
01:17:52.970 --> 01:17:54.750
One more time while he's venting.

1530
01:17:54.750 --> 01:17:56.350
Yes, exactly.

1531
01:17:56.710 --> 01:18:05.550
Well, you guys, part of Kent's job, he has such a hard job because he answers to both Greg, who is the creative voice of the show.

1532
01:18:05.770 --> 01:18:10.090
And, you know, his job is to make all of Greg's dreams come true creatively.

1533
01:18:10.190 --> 01:18:15.570
But he also answers to the network who has given him a budget and a timetable.

1534
01:18:15.570 --> 01:18:17.470
And you can't spend more money than this.

1535
01:18:17.470 --> 01:18:25.830
And everything that you said earlier, Angela, about staying on time, because Kent will look at a script and he will try to estimate,

1536
01:18:25.830 --> 01:18:31.730
based on conversations with the director and the writer and the crew, how long each scene will take.

1537
01:18:31.730 --> 01:18:36.110
So he'll say, all right, we need three hours to film Ed at sea.

1538
01:18:36.530 --> 01:18:40.670
But I'm only going to give 45 minutes to Jim and Karen in the sand.

1539
01:18:40.850 --> 01:18:41.410
That's right.

1540
01:18:41.510 --> 01:18:46.970
And if any of these things go over even 10 minutes, they have to steal 10 minutes from another scene.

1541
01:18:47.230 --> 01:18:50.130
And it becomes just this logistical nightmare.

1542
01:18:50.410 --> 01:18:52.370
And Kent has to keep an eye on all of it.

1543
01:18:52.510 --> 01:18:55.050
And it is a vicious domino effect, too.

1544
01:18:55.050 --> 01:18:57.990
Once you get off, it just spirals your whole day.

1545
01:18:58.370 --> 01:19:07.190
I feel like this is like a great metaphor for partnerships, because I feel like in every family, there's one person that's a little bit of the big thinker dreamer.

1546
01:19:07.710 --> 01:19:09.810
On vacation, we'll do all of this.

1547
01:19:09.910 --> 01:19:16.550
And then there's the person in the relationship that's like, I've allotted $80 for things at the gift shop.

1548
01:19:16.730 --> 01:19:18.490
That's all we're spending at the gift shop, OK?

1549
01:19:18.490 --> 01:19:25.270
OK, because if we spend more than $80 at the gift shop, we can't do that extra bungee cord thing that you all want to do.

1550
01:19:26.730 --> 01:19:29.110
Yes, that is exactly Kent.

1551
01:19:29.410 --> 01:19:30.550
He is that person.

1552
01:19:31.370 --> 01:19:32.530
Keep us on budget.

1553
01:19:32.770 --> 01:19:33.690
Keep us on time.

1554
01:19:33.850 --> 01:19:34.010
Yep.

1555
01:19:34.790 --> 01:19:38.750
Well, now, Angela, it is time for the Coal Walk.

1556
01:19:38.930 --> 01:19:40.270
Talk about a big scene.

1557
01:19:40.450 --> 01:19:42.250
This was a big evening for us.

1558
01:19:42.530 --> 01:19:44.430
It was a very ambitious shoot.

1559
01:19:44.630 --> 01:19:45.530
We had a lot to cover.

1560
01:19:45.690 --> 01:19:48.030
We could not start till it got dark.

1561
01:19:48.610 --> 01:19:49.170
Yep.

1562
01:19:49.630 --> 01:19:51.870
And there was a lot of prep.

1563
01:19:52.110 --> 01:19:54.890
I don't know if you remember, Jenna, how they made those coals.

1564
01:19:55.070 --> 01:19:55.470
Do you remember?

1565
01:19:55.770 --> 01:19:56.450
I do.

1566
01:19:56.630 --> 01:19:58.010
It was multi-layered.

1567
01:19:58.170 --> 01:20:03.290
And we had a lot of people ask this question about how did you make the coals?

1568
01:20:03.410 --> 01:20:10.730
We got mail from Ellie Troutman, Chandra M., Jamie S., Heather Donnelly, Renee R., and many others.

1569
01:20:11.230 --> 01:20:16.290
They wanted to know how did the props department create the hot coals?

1570
01:20:16.410 --> 01:20:17.750
They looked so real.

1571
01:20:17.750 --> 01:20:20.150
Well, because, guys, these were not real coals.

1572
01:20:20.450 --> 01:20:20.650
Yes.

1573
01:20:20.770 --> 01:20:23.290
So we could not have a huge fire here, you guys.

1574
01:20:23.430 --> 01:20:26.610
We were allowed to have the tiki torches and little small flames.

1575
01:20:26.810 --> 01:20:32.310
For example, when the pile of wood burst into flames, that's all special effects.

1576
01:20:32.550 --> 01:20:34.170
That was not at all a fire.

1577
01:20:34.410 --> 01:20:40.470
They put that in in post because there were fire regulations at this particular park, right?

1578
01:20:40.470 --> 01:20:44.590
Well, there are fire regulations in California in general.

1579
01:20:44.770 --> 01:20:45.110
Yes.

1580
01:20:45.310 --> 01:20:46.090
Yes, absolutely.

1581
01:20:46.570 --> 01:20:51.690
So Harold Ramis talked a little bit about this coal fire pit on the DVD commentary.

1582
01:20:51.790 --> 01:20:57.970
He said they started, first of all, by digging a big hole in the ground.

1583
01:20:57.970 --> 01:21:05.110
And then they buried in the ground a light box, just a big box that lit up.

1584
01:21:05.210 --> 01:21:12.850
And then they put on top of the light box these little yellow and orange gels to make it look

1585
01:21:12.850 --> 01:21:13.530
like a flame.

1586
01:21:13.530 --> 01:21:20.590
And then on top of those light gels, on top of the light box, they put little lava rocks.

1587
01:21:20.590 --> 01:21:27.930
And then running on either side of this hole in the ground, they had put two gas lines with

1588
01:21:27.930 --> 01:21:32.450
very small little flickers of real fire that popped out of the ground.

1589
01:21:33.310 --> 01:21:40.610
Yes, and because there was real fire as part of the coal walk, we had a very big safety meeting

1590
01:21:40.610 --> 01:21:41.230
about that.

1591
01:21:41.390 --> 01:21:42.270
One of our biggeSt.

1592
01:21:42.830 --> 01:21:45.150
Yeah, for sure.

1593
01:21:45.410 --> 01:21:52.670
It's particularly important because, as you know, Rain, as Dwight, is going to walk onto

1594
01:21:52.670 --> 01:21:55.490
these hot coals, stand there, and then fall to the ground.

1595
01:21:55.490 --> 01:21:57.670
And that was real fire next to him.

1596
01:21:57.790 --> 01:21:59.330
On either side of him, yes.

1597
01:21:59.330 --> 01:22:04.550
And I remember when Rain did that, one of the most painful things for Rain was being barefoot

1598
01:22:04.550 --> 01:22:05.610
on the lava rocks.

1599
01:22:05.850 --> 01:22:06.450
It hurt.

1600
01:22:06.630 --> 01:22:08.450
It's like stepping on a Lego.

1601
01:22:08.610 --> 01:22:09.530
You know how that feels.

1602
01:22:09.870 --> 01:22:12.670
I do know how it feels because I did it as well.

1603
01:22:12.810 --> 01:22:13.010
Yeah.

1604
01:22:13.210 --> 01:22:19.190
In fact, I'll say that people have mentioned that I look like my feet really hurt after

1605
01:22:19.190 --> 01:22:23.250
I walk across the hot coals and they've wondered if I really walked on hot coals.

1606
01:22:23.250 --> 01:22:29.990
No, my feet were sore and bruised from having to run across lava rocks over and over again.

1607
01:22:30.110 --> 01:22:30.230
Yeah.

1608
01:22:30.390 --> 01:22:31.950
Like my feet did kind of hurt.

1609
01:22:32.230 --> 01:22:36.590
Throw a bunch of like hard Legos on the ground and run back and forth over them.

1610
01:22:37.070 --> 01:22:39.030
That's as good as a coal walk right there.

1611
01:22:39.650 --> 01:22:40.830
A Lego walk.

1612
01:22:41.090 --> 01:22:43.830
If you want that experience, you can do it at home.

1613
01:22:44.250 --> 01:22:46.670
Michael wanted everyone to walk over the hot coals.

1614
01:22:46.670 --> 01:22:52.470
We said he has to go first and Steve's hesitation as Michael to go over those coals, Jenna, was

1615
01:22:52.470 --> 01:22:53.130
so brilliant.

1616
01:22:53.450 --> 01:23:01.230
His whole body posture, the expression on his face, and he improvised the line, the mind

1617
01:23:01.230 --> 01:23:03.230
has to wrap around the foot.

1618
01:23:04.450 --> 01:23:08.610
That was an improvisation.

1619
01:23:08.730 --> 01:23:09.750
I did not look that up.

1620
01:23:09.870 --> 01:23:10.850
I love knowing that.

1621
01:23:11.070 --> 01:23:16.570
And we have a call back to this whole runner of Angela being grossed out by Kevin's feet.

1622
01:23:17.510 --> 01:23:18.110
Yes.

1623
01:23:18.110 --> 01:23:22.010
She's like, I am not going to go after you and your gross feet.

1624
01:23:22.190 --> 01:23:24.810
And he's like, Angela, it's like a thousand degrees.

1625
01:23:26.770 --> 01:23:33.930
One of my favorite moments in this whole sequence is when Michael says to the group, he's so

1626
01:23:33.930 --> 01:23:39.050
disappointed that no one has walked across the coals except sort of Dwight.

1627
01:23:39.730 --> 01:23:42.190
And he says, you know what?

1628
01:23:42.610 --> 01:23:47.410
It's clear to me that if I had to name my replacement today, it would be Mr. Outside

1629
01:23:47.410 --> 01:23:47.830
Higher.

1630
01:23:48.130 --> 01:23:51.730
And then Angela says, or Mrs. Outside Higher.

1631
01:23:51.990 --> 01:23:55.930
And Michael is like, yes, or that.

1632
01:23:56.550 --> 01:23:58.270
He's so condescending.

1633
01:23:58.270 --> 01:24:04.060
I know, but a little bit of Angela sass there.

1634
01:24:04.400 --> 01:24:05.480
Like, I pointed my finger.

1635
01:24:06.360 --> 01:24:06.880
Yeah.

1636
01:24:07.540 --> 01:24:10.480
Wait, Jenna, we do have a background catch.

1637
01:24:10.620 --> 01:24:11.540
We can't pass up.

1638
01:24:11.620 --> 01:24:12.320
It's too good.

1639
01:24:12.820 --> 01:24:13.420
Say it.

1640
01:24:13.520 --> 01:24:17.300
While Michael's going on and on about who won't walk across the coals, and he's like,

1641
01:24:17.480 --> 01:24:18.900
and Andy is never here today.

1642
01:24:19.360 --> 01:24:21.980
It pans past us to the water.

1643
01:24:23.040 --> 01:24:25.020
Andy has floated into the marsh.

1644
01:24:25.020 --> 01:24:29.780
He's stuck on his back, poor Ed, in the dark, in the lake.

1645
01:24:29.980 --> 01:24:35.400
And at 19 minutes, 55 seconds, you can see his wetsuit on his arm.

1646
01:24:35.560 --> 01:24:35.660
Oh!

1647
01:24:35.860 --> 01:24:36.780
Yeah, on his arm.

1648
01:24:36.780 --> 01:24:42.580
His arm is, like, extended out of his sumo suit, and you can see the beige wetsuit that

1649
01:24:42.580 --> 01:24:44.500
he had to put on because it was so cold.

1650
01:24:44.960 --> 01:24:53.160
And then people wrote in, Angela, and they said, how come in season nine, when Pam is crying,

1651
01:24:53.560 --> 01:25:02.700
the documentary crew helps her, but when Andy is floating away at night, they just turn off

1652
01:25:02.700 --> 01:25:04.640
the camera lights and walk away.

1653
01:25:04.640 --> 01:25:05.340
I know.

1654
01:25:05.860 --> 01:25:06.720
I know.

1655
01:25:06.960 --> 01:25:09.060
That's one of the questions Jack had.

1656
01:25:09.200 --> 01:25:12.700
My stepson, he was like, why did the camera crew not help him?

1657
01:25:13.200 --> 01:25:13.840
I know.

1658
01:25:13.920 --> 01:25:14.980
And I was like, you know what?

1659
01:25:15.040 --> 01:25:20.380
At that moment, they were like, we can't disturb the natural course of the story.

1660
01:25:20.640 --> 01:25:20.780
Right.

1661
01:25:20.780 --> 01:25:22.260
We must just document it.

1662
01:25:23.220 --> 01:25:25.520
Well, we're coming up to this big moment.

1663
01:25:26.080 --> 01:25:28.720
There is a huge moment about to happen for Pam.

1664
01:25:29.360 --> 01:25:29.840
Yeah.

1665
01:25:29.960 --> 01:25:32.520
She wants to run across the coals, and Michael says, no.

1666
01:25:32.840 --> 01:25:33.260
No.

1667
01:25:33.860 --> 01:25:34.320
Yeah.

1668
01:25:34.320 --> 01:25:38.280
And then he moves them on, and he wants them all to speak.

1669
01:25:38.520 --> 01:25:42.060
Dwight is doing his version of the aristocrats very badly.

1670
01:25:42.940 --> 01:25:46.580
And in the background, Pam runs across the coals.

1671
01:25:47.520 --> 01:25:48.120
Yeah.

1672
01:25:48.580 --> 01:25:51.120
And this just feeds her soul.

1673
01:25:51.360 --> 01:25:53.900
She feels so alive in this moment.

1674
01:25:54.200 --> 01:25:56.580
She did this one thing for herself.

1675
01:25:56.580 --> 01:26:01.020
And she runs over to the group, and she wants to share.

1676
01:26:02.360 --> 01:26:08.560
And she gives this speech, and everything just comes pouring out.

1677
01:26:08.760 --> 01:26:12.440
Her disappointment that people didn't come to the art show.

1678
01:26:12.440 --> 01:26:26.320
But more than that, her feelings for Jim and how much she misses him, and the truth, the big truth, which is that while there were many reasons to call off her wedding to Roy,

1679
01:26:26.680 --> 01:26:31.460
the reason she did it was because of how she feels about Jim.

1680
01:26:31.460 --> 01:26:32.160
Yeah.

1681
01:26:33.540 --> 01:26:34.280
Yeah.

1682
01:27:01.360 --> 01:27:01.420
And oof.

1683
01:27:01.420 --> 01:27:05.520
And I had to watch you as Pam transition through all of those beats.

1684
01:27:05.520 --> 01:27:21.640
And I had this out-of-body experience as your friend watching you in awe give this fantastic performance, wanting to tear up and cry because my friend was crushing it.

1685
01:27:21.640 --> 01:27:27.880
And then having to be Angela Martin, who would never react to you that way.

1686
01:27:28.640 --> 01:27:33.960
And then at the same time, just being in awe of this moment.

1687
01:27:33.980 --> 01:27:37.080
It was just a beautiful thing to watch, Jenna.

1688
01:27:37.220 --> 01:27:40.120
You did such a great job, and you had to do it over and over.

1689
01:27:40.240 --> 01:27:43.380
We did several takes because they had to get people's reactions.

1690
01:27:43.540 --> 01:27:46.600
And every time, you crushed it.

1691
01:27:46.760 --> 01:27:47.920
Every single time.

1692
01:27:49.040 --> 01:27:56.660
Well, a lot of people ask if that speech was improvised or partially improvised, and it was not.

1693
01:27:56.960 --> 01:28:02.000
That was all written, and as Jen shared with us, written by Greg Daniels.

1694
01:28:02.120 --> 01:28:05.880
And I memorized it and delivered it word for word.

1695
01:28:06.940 --> 01:28:19.040
And I worked on it for a very long time, and I remember trying to think about those times in my life where you've done something that give you a burst of adrenaline.

1696
01:28:22.040 --> 01:28:26.080
And that's what I wanted her to come into the speech with.

1697
01:28:26.080 --> 01:28:36.720
She's just done this cold walk, and she has this burst of adrenaline, and she doesn't even think about that she's going to share.

1698
01:28:36.940 --> 01:28:38.280
It's completely different.

1699
01:28:38.420 --> 01:28:43.920
This is very impulsive, and that was kind of what I put behind the speech.

1700
01:28:44.060 --> 01:28:47.060
But I knew that speech backwards and forwards.

1701
01:28:47.060 --> 01:28:58.400
I could have delivered it for days because I also know, as an actor, at least for me especially, the more I know the material, the less I have to think of the word.

1702
01:28:58.620 --> 01:28:58.980
Exactly.

1703
01:28:59.680 --> 01:29:01.920
And it can just be about the performance.

1704
01:29:01.920 --> 01:29:04.100
I never had to search for it.

1705
01:29:04.220 --> 01:29:04.800
I had it.

1706
01:29:04.880 --> 01:29:07.320
It was like on a little tape in my head.

1707
01:29:07.700 --> 01:29:08.900
That's exactly right.

1708
01:29:08.900 --> 01:29:15.840
You don't have to worry about what you're saying, and you can just really experience the moment and feel it because the words are all there.

1709
01:29:16.460 --> 01:29:27.060
And I just thought it was brilliant, and I thought everyone's reactions were great because there is a dance that you do on a show like The Office.

1710
01:29:27.060 --> 01:29:34.860
There is the person who is driving the scene, who's putting out all this information, and then the other side of the story is the people reacting to it.

1711
01:29:35.060 --> 01:29:37.040
And the two parts are very important.

1712
01:29:37.040 --> 01:29:39.580
And I thought that John is Jim.

1713
01:29:39.860 --> 01:29:40.840
His reactions were great.

1714
01:29:41.020 --> 01:29:44.060
Everyone in the crowd, they cut to Rashida.

1715
01:29:44.440 --> 01:29:45.440
Oh, my God.

1716
01:29:45.620 --> 01:29:48.640
Her look as Karen was amazing.

1717
01:29:48.860 --> 01:29:51.580
It was like, I can't believe this is happening.

1718
01:29:51.900 --> 01:29:52.840
Oh, my God.

1719
01:29:53.140 --> 01:29:54.320
It was everything.

1720
01:29:54.880 --> 01:29:56.520
And also heartbreak.

1721
01:29:56.740 --> 01:29:57.760
It was all of it.

1722
01:29:58.780 --> 01:29:58.920
Yeah.

1723
01:29:59.520 --> 01:30:04.300
Well, and you mention about what it's like to be the person driving a scene.

1724
01:30:04.300 --> 01:30:12.180
This was kind of my first experience with that, and it was this huge speech, and that's another reason why I made sure I knew every single word.

1725
01:30:12.520 --> 01:30:17.780
And I got a real glimpse at what Steve does every single day, every single week.

1726
01:30:19.060 --> 01:30:19.500
Wow.

1727
01:30:20.620 --> 01:30:22.660
Delivering these big speeches to the group.

1728
01:30:22.660 --> 01:30:24.680
I mean, all your eyeballs were on me.

1729
01:30:24.780 --> 01:30:25.560
I was so nervous.

1730
01:30:26.340 --> 01:30:26.460
Yeah.

1731
01:30:26.560 --> 01:30:27.480
It's a lot of work.

1732
01:30:27.480 --> 01:30:28.560
It's a lot of work.

1733
01:30:28.700 --> 01:30:32.180
